    48	
    49	\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
    50	\paragraph{Verification-based probation.} Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a worker's verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; follow-on work treats imperfect checks~\cite{watanabe2010generalized}. BOINC's adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and trusts a host above a threshold~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing gold and peer evaluation~\cite{qiu2018crowdeval}. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}, and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close supervision of new firms~\cite{gangl2014supervision}. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the track record~\cite{bhaskar2025screening}. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit quota (Section~\ref{sec:rules}).
    51	
    52	\paragraph{Cheap pseudonyms.} With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price of cooperation~\cite{friedman2001social}; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high~\cite{feldman2006free}; without a central authority one attacker can create many identities~\cite{douceur2002sybil}. Reputation mechanisms with pure moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in~\cite{dellarocas2005reputation}. Reputation without persistent identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in~\cite{gatta2026tempting}; that model has no verification budget.
    53	
    54	\paragraph{Costly verification and audits.} Costly state verification goes back to~\cite{townsend1979optimal}; history-dependent audit groups under restricted penalties go back to~\cite{harrington1988enforcement}; allocation with costly verification~\cite{benporath2014optimal}, limited punishments~\cite{li2020mechanism}, financially constrained agents~\cite{li2021financially}, and ex post verification with limited penalties~\cite{mylovanov2017optimal} assume persistent agents. Recursive inspection games spend a finite stock of inspections~\cite{vonstengel2016recursive}; audit games optimise randomised inspection against a best-responding attacker~\cite{blocki2013audit}; targeted and untargeted inspection under fixed capacity~\cite{liu2013enforcement} and dynamic monitoring under resource constraints~\cite{solan2021dynamic} are also studied; optimal committed audit policies against the worst equilibrium of the audited agents, with budgeted variants, are derived in~\cite{das2026optimally}. Tournament-based dynamic targeting allocates a fixed inspection capacity by relative compliance rank and compares it with standards-based and random inspection~\cite{gilpatric2015competition}; R3 ranks by identity age instead. Our rules are searched and tuned heuristics, not optima of these models. Paid computation with verification by recomputation and colluding workers is analysed in~\cite{fernandezanta2015algorithmic}, and cheating in outsourced distributed computation in~\cite{golle2001uncheatable}.
    55	
    56	\paragraph{Verifiable computation.} Proofs that a computation was executed as committed now reach large language models. FlashProve proves, after the fact, that a committed integer version of a 400-billion-parameter model produced a token as its greedy choice; its Lean proofs cover the protocol, while the executable prover and verifier were generated by AI and are not proved equivalent to it, and its technical report states that it was AI-generated and not reviewed in detail~\cite{rinberg2026flashprove}. Such proofs check that a committed computation ran; they do not check the quality of an output, which is what our audits observe.
    57	
    58	\paragraph{Evaluation method.} Simulation-based strategic analysis has an established method~\cite{wellman2006methods,wellman2025egta}, including bootstrap inference for empirical games~\cite{wiedenbeck2014bootstrap}. Weak or incomplete attacks overstate robustness, and adaptive evaluations must be tailored to each defence~\cite{carlini2019evaluating,tramer2020adaptive}. Preregistration separates generating a hypothesis from testing it on new observations~\cite{nosek2018preregistration}. Our staged registration applies these tools.
    59	
    60	\section{The market model}\label{sec:model}
    61	\paragraph{Market.} Time is discrete, $t = 1, \dots, T$, with $T = 200$ in both tests except the first test's language-model attacker ($T = 100$). In each round $J = 50$ unit jobs arrive; job $j$ has value $v_j$ per unit of delivered quality, drawn independently and uniformly on $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}]$. There are $N = 10$ providers; provider $i$ has a quality ceiling $q^{\max}_i$, a per-job cost $a_i + b_i q$ at effort $q$, a capacity of $k_i$ jobs per round, and an outside option of zero. The provider population and the market parameters of each test are stated in Appendix~\ref{sec:app-params} and Section~\ref{sec:design}.
    62	
    63	\paragraph{Offers, effort, delivery.} In each round an active provider privately chooses an effort $q_i \in [0, q^{\max}_i]$ for all its jobs and submits a public offer: a claim $c_i \in [0,1]$ (a promise that expected delivered quality is at least $c_i$) and a per-unit bid. A served job delivers $y = q_i + \varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2)$, independently across jobs; the buyer's value is $v_j y$. Delivering below the claim is shading.
    64	
    65	\paragraph{Rounds and audits.} The rule allocates jobs and computes payments, which buyers fund into escrow. After delivery the rule selects jobs to audit; an audit reveals $y$ exactly at cost $\alpha$. A job fails if $y < c_i - \tau$, with $\tau$ the rule's tolerance. The rule imposes consequences, and escrow is settled.
    66	
    67	\paragraph{Audit budget.} Let $\hat S$ be the expected honest first-best surplus over $T$ rounds and $m = \hat S / n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator's audit budget is $B = \beta \hat S$ and the audit cost $\alpha = \rho m$, so the allowance is $B/\alpha = (\beta/\rho)\, n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ audits; unspent budget carries over.
    68	
    69	\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. In the registered tests the honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Section~\ref{sec:churn} relaxes this: honest opponents replace their identities at a fixed rate.
    70	
    71	\paragraph{Admissible rules.} A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought, its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only consequences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity's receipts as a bond; and allocation priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.
    72	
    73	\paragraph{Benchmark and metrics.} One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly: full effort $q_i = q^{\max}_i$, the truthful claim $c_i = q^{\max}_i$, and a bid equal to their delivery cost $a_i + b_i q^{\max}_i$; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best surplus $S^*$, the focal provider's \emph{gain} is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected truthful reference, divided by $S^*/N$. The truthful reference is the policy with the highest mean normalised training profit in the rule's menu of truthful candidate policies; it need not be full-effort play and may replace identities. \emph{Gross efficiency} is $(W + \text{audit cost})/S^*$, with $W$ the realised social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is $W/S^*$. Gain is a difference of two searched values, so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity; Section~\ref{sec:reset} reports an exploratory matched comparison with replacement disabled, within the D2 family only. The second test adds two objectives at its headline market (the first test's market; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-params}): welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker's reference on the training seeds (W1), whose profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95\% lower bound on the evaluation seeds is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain $G_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and by the worst member's mean gain $G_{\min}$, both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results say nothing about coalition stability.
    74	
    75	\section{The audit rules we compare}\label{sec:rules}
    76	\paragraph{Baselines.} B2 posts prices for three claim tiers, allocates by those prices, and audits uniformly. B3 allocates each job, in descending order of value, to the offer with remaining capacity and the highest nonnegative score $v_j c_i - \mathrm{bid}_i$, pays $v_j c_i - s_{2,j}$ with $s_{2,j}$ the highest score among the other eligible offers (zero if there is none), and audits uniformly at random among served jobs, spread over the rounds. Both refund a failed job and ban its identity for $L$ rounds.
    77	
    78	\paragraph{R3.} R3 keeps B3's allocation, payment, tolerance, and enforcement and changes only which jobs it audits and when. \emph{Pacing:} with the integer allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, R3 reserves $\min(JL, A)$ audits for the last $L$ rounds, in which it audits every served job while audits remain, and spends the rest evenly over the earlier rounds, carrying unspent quota forward. \emph{Selection:} it first audits the served jobs of identities younger than $L$ rounds, newest first; it then spends $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ of the $r$ remaining audits on the newest established identities and the rest uniformly at random.
    79	
    80	\paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
    81	
    82	\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at the development instance dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
    83	
    84	\paragraph{The repair R4 and tuned variants.} R4 is R3 with the terminal reserve capped at $\min(JL, \lfloor A/2 \rfloor)$, and C$^*$/S2 is C$^*$ with that schedule. They differ from R3 and C$^*$ only when $JL > \lfloor A/2 \rfloor$: they equal them in the second test's blocks at $\rho = \const{0.2}$ and appear only in its repair block at $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's reserve consumes the whole allowance. Separately tuned versions of B3, R3, and C$^*$ (B3-t, R3-t, C$^*$-t), each over a common grid of \const{16} tolerance and ban-length pairs and so possibly at other pairs than the fixed one, are a secondary comparison.
    85	
    86	\section{What the theory shows}\label{sec:theory}
    87	We fix a provider facing honest opponents and an offer with claim $0 < c \le q^{\max}$, and compare truthful effort $c$ with effort $c - \Delta$, $0 \le \Delta \le c$, at the unchanged offer. We condition on the pre-delivery state, rival outcomes, and the audit set, all independent of the focal delivery noise, and assume that focal audit outcomes affect continuation value only through whether any focal job fails, with the same conditional values under both effort levels. Shading saves $g = b\Delta$ per served job and raises the per-audit failure probability from $d_0 = \Phi(-\tau/\sigma)$ to $d = \Phi((\Delta - \tau)/\sigma)$. Let $s$ be the provider's served jobs in the round, $U$ the audited subset with $K = |U|$ and escrows $M_j$, and $C_U$ the difference between the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one.
    88	
    89	\begin{lemma}[local identity]\label{lem:local}
    90	Under these assumptions, the expected profit of shading by $\Delta$ relative to truthful effort is
    91	\[ A(\Delta) = s g - \mathbb{E}\Big[(d - d_0)\textstyle\sum_{j \in U} M_j + \big((1-d_0)^K - (1-d)^K\big) C_U\Big]. \]
    92	\end{lemma}
    93	
    94	\begin{corollary}[uniform auditing under replacement]\label{cor:uniform}
    95	If, after an immediate replacement at $e = 0$, the distribution of all payoff-relevant future states equals that after no failure up to identity relabelling, and every continuation available after a failure is also available after no failure up to relabelling (both hold for B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation), then $C_U = 0$, and shading pays whenever $sg > (d - d_0)\,\mathbb{E}\sum_{j \in U} M_j$, whatever the ban length.
    96	\end{corollary}
    97	
    98	\begin{lemma}[coverage]\label{lem:coverage}
    99	R3 audits every served job of identities younger than $L$ rounds (age counted from the first offer) whenever the round's audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited by age group, newest first, with $k$ the round's available audit count; an identity that offers but wins no jobs still ages; within the focal identity's group of $n$ jobs, of which $s$ are its own, the number audited is hypergeometric with $k' = \min\{n, \max(0, k - H)\}$ draws, $H$ being the fresh jobs of strictly newer identities.
   100	\end{lemma}
   101	
   102	After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn the uniquely-newest condition may no longer hold, so the guarantee may not apply, and coverage can be zero if newer jobs exhaust the relevant quota (Section~\ref{sec:churn}).
   103	
   104	
   105	
   106	Appendix~\ref{app:proofs} gives a conditional renewal bound on a replacing attacker's long-run excess profit per round under R3 (Theorem~\ref{thm:renewal}); its assumptions are not established for the implemented process, and no reported result depends on it.
   107	
   108	\begin{proposition}[terminal shading]\label{prop:terminal}
   109	In a round with zero continuation loss, a served provider starting from a positive truthful effort gains from small same-offer shading whenever $b > v_{\max}\,\phi(\tau/\sigma)/\sigma$.
   110	\end{proposition}
   111	
   112	At the first test's parameters the right-hand side is below $\const{0.067}$ and every provider has $b \ge \const{0.22}$, so no studied rule eliminates gain; all claims are bounded-gain claims. Proofs are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}; the theory checks of the first test are in the supplement.
   113	
   114	\section{The attacks we test against}\label{sec:attacks}
   115	Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
