   137	\section{The second test: design and registration}\label{sec:design}
   138	\paragraph{The first test.} The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before R3 was implemented. The original claim failed because neither uniform-audit baseline met its criterion against the sealed family; an amended claim, written after development, passed. Appendix~\ref{sec:eval1} gives the verdict, the erratum, and the failure modes it found.
   139	
   140	\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.
   141	
   142	\paragraph{Lock and draw.} The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, manifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged \texttt{protocol-v2}, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bitcoin tip was at block \const{974379}. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block $A = \const{974307}$; the draw block is $H = A + \const{144} = \const{974451}$, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after $H$, the draw would have been kept and its results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at $H$ to influence this evaluation; a later reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.
   143	
   144	\paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.
   145	
   146	\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (\const{9999} resamples) that resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules' outcomes on each seed; this measures uncertainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain is $g = (\pi^{A} - \pi^{T})/(S^*/N)$, attacked profit minus the profit of the rule's frozen truthful reference, normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with $N = \const{10}$ providers, the Q1 margin of $\const{0.05}$ is half a percent of the first-best surplus. \emph{Calibration.} Interval tails are scaled by a multiplier $\kappa$ admitted on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}); admission does not cover search or reference selection and does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Conditional on valid calibrated $p$-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six Q4 decisions separately; Q1's categories follow equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}.
   147	
   148	\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
   149	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.78}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
   150	Question & Rule \\\midrule
   151	Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
   152	Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
   153	Q2, Q3 & Registered analyses descriptive, with nominal intervals and extrema selected on training data; the post hoc lottery analysis selects its companion and weight on evaluation data. \\\bottomrule
