   217	\paragraph{Ordering and audits on the new markets.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad5} and negative on \site{rp_ad5_n} of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is \site{rp_au5} (negative on \site{rp_au5_n}), and B3 minus R3 is \site{rp_aq5}, negative on \site{rp_aq5_n} markets (\site{rpf_aq5_n} with the all-frozen winners). The sign reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the primary winners all three market $t$ intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table~\ref{tab:replication}); the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [\site{rp_aq5_blo}, \site{rp_aq5_bhi}] (Table~\ref{tab:rep-absolute}). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad3} ([\site{rp_ad3_lo}, \site{rp_ad3_hi}]) and negative on only \site{rp_ad3_n} of six markets (\site{rpf_ad3_n} with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs falls from between \site{rp_fa0_lo} and \site{rp_fa0_hi} without churn to between \site{rp_fa5_lo} and \site{rp_fa5_hi} at $p = \const{0.05}$, and is lower on \site{rp_fa_below} of six markets. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{rp_yj5} of the jobs and receive \site{rp_ya5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{rp_est5} (equal-weight means over the six markets; Table~\ref{tab:rep-alloc}). At $p = \const{0.03}$ the primary allocation covers only \site{rp_al3_n} of six markets: the run code computed diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at $p = \const{0}$ and \const{0.05}, so rep2, rep3, and rep4, whose R3 winners at \const{0.03} come from continued search, have none; this is a gap against the plan, which asks for the allocation at every rate. With the primary winners, R3's selected attack at $p = \const{0.05}$ replaces its identity on \site{rp_x5} of six markets and keeps one identity on the others (\site{rpf_x5} with the all-frozen winners). Under its selected attack at \const{0.05}, R3's mean gross efficiency is \site{rp_eff5} (lowest market \site{rp_eff5_min}) and C$^*$'s is \site{rp_effc5} (Table~\ref{tab:rep-terms}).
   218	
   219	\paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, the pooled point estimates show no measured advantage of R3 over C$^*$: the comparison is unresolved at the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up and negative from $p = \const{0.03}$; the pooled point estimate of R3's advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
