-\paragraph{Prospective-exploratory replication on new markets.} After two external reviews of this section, we planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test's market generator. The replication runs R3, C$^*$, B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(\const{12}) at $p = \const{0}$, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05}, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule's selected no-churn reference candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell, and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling check was flagged. Of the \const{72} cells, \site{rp_cont} were flagged and continued (\site{rp_cont0} at $p = \const{0}$, \site{rp_cont3} at \const{0.03}, and \site{rp_cont5} at \const{0.05}; \site{rp_cont_r3} of them R3's), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds; passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell's frozen winner unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired change from the same market's no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion requires a mean change at or below the registered Q1 margin of $-\const{0.05}$; both are reported for both winner sets. The fourth expectation carried a stated prospective probability of \const{0.5}. For the fifth, R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is the number of the attacker's audited jobs over its served jobs, each summed over a market's \const{200} evaluation seeds;