iginal failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper (see Availability).
+For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. Under the prescribed searches and without honest churn, R3 gives the attacker a lower measured gain than uniform audits on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, and R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin. Within the prescribed D2 searches, most of R3's measured advantage over uniform audits disappears when attacker replacement is disabled. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers: the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and at $p = \const{0.05}$ the pooled point estimates of C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are \site{c_ch_d5} and \site{c_ch_q5}. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the same deterioration from no churn against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits under its descriptive criterion; at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on \site{c_rp_dn} and \site{c_rp_qn} of the new markets, but at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{c_rp_d3n}, the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The practical reading is narrow: at the tested parameters, checking the newest identities first lowers the measured gain of an attacker that is the main source of new identities, and once honest turnover supplies most new identities, the pooled point estimates of that advantage are negative against C$^*$ from $p = \const{0.03}$ and against uniform audits at \const{0.05}, with most market-level intervals including zero. All follow-up intervals are exploratory and omit search uncertainty. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper (see Availability).
