206: \end{figure}
207: 
208: \paragraph{Ordering and audits on the new markets.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad5} and negative on \site{rp_ad5_n} of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is \site{rp_au5} (negative on \site{rp_au5_n}), and B3 minus R3 is \site{rp_aq5}, negative on \site{rp_aq5_n} markets (\site{rpf_aq5_n} with the all-frozen winners). The sign reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the primary winners all three market $t$ intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table~\ref{tab:replication}); the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [\site{rp_aq5_blo}, \site{rp_aq5_bhi}] (Table~\ref{tab:rep-absolute}). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad3} ([\site{rp_ad3_lo}, \site{rp_ad3_hi}]) and negative on only \site{rp_ad3_n} of six markets (\site{rpf_ad3_n} with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs falls from between \site{rp_fa0_lo} and \site{rp_fa0_hi} without churn to between \site{rp_fa5_lo} and \site{rp_fa5_hi} at $p = \const{0.05}$, and is lower on \site{rp_fa_below} of six markets. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{rp_yj5} of the jobs and receive \site{rp_ya5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{rp_est5} (equal-weight means over the six markets; Table~\ref{tab:rep-alloc}). At $p = \const{0.03}$ the primary allocation covers only \site{rp_al3_n} of six markets: the run code computed diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at $p = \const{0}$ and \const{0.05}, so rep2, rep3, and rep4, whose R3 winners at \const{0.03} come from continued search, have none; this is a gap against the plan, which asks for the allocation at every rate. With the primary winners, R3's selected attack at $p = \const{0.05}$ replaces its identity on \site{rp_x5} of six markets and keeps one identity on the others (\site{rpf_x5} with the all-frozen winners). Under its selected attack at \const{0.05}, R3's mean gross efficiency is \site{rp_eff5} (lowest market \site{rp_eff5_min}) and C$^*$'s is \site{rp_effc5} (Table~\ref{tab:rep-terms}).
209: 
210: \paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, the pooled point estimates show no measured advantage of R3 over C$^*$: the comparison is unresolved at the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up and negative from $p = \const{0.03}$; the pooled point estimate of R3's advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
211: 

LINES 230 243
230: \paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled point estimate of R3's advantage over C$^*$ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative from \const{0.03}, and that of its advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest rate, \const{0.05} (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); most market-level intervals include zero; that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the two lowest rates. Its prospective-exploratory replication on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, its market-level intervals mostly include zero, and its run code computed no diagnostics for continued R3 winners at $p = \const{0.03}$, so R3's audit allocation there covers only three of six markets, a gap against its plan. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
231: 
232: \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
233: % BEGIN-EV2
234: For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. Under the prescribed searches and without honest churn, R3 gives the attacker a lower measured gain than uniform audits on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, and R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin. Within the prescribed D2 searches, most of R3's measured advantage over uniform audits disappears when attacker replacement is disabled. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers: the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and at $p = \const{0.05}$ the pooled point estimates of C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are \site{c_ch_d5} and \site{c_ch_q5}. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the same deterioration from no churn against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits under its descriptive criterion; at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on \site{c_rp_dn} and \site{c_rp_qn} of the new markets, but at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{c_rp_d3n}, the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The practical reading is narrow: at the tested parameters, checking the newest identities first lowers the measured gain of an attacker that is the main source of new identities, and once honest turnover supplies most new identities, the pooled point estimates of that advantage are negative against C$^*$ from $p = \const{0.03}$ and against uniform audits at \const{0.05}, with most market-level intervals including zero. All follow-up intervals are exploratory and omit search uncertainty. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper (see Availability).
235: % END-EV2
236: 
237: \paragraph{Availability.} The repository is public at \url{https://github.com/demirelo/touchstone-registered-tests} (release v2.1, archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24498973}), and its eight record files, with their SHA-\const{256} hashes, are archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24499068}. The release identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, the identity-replacement and churn follow-ups with their run records, the prospective-exploratory replication with its timestamped plan and manifest, block-hash draw, run records, analysis, and the record digest that its paper extract is checked against, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, \texttt{docs/revision/followup2}, and \texttt{docs/revision/followup3}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}. For review, an anonymized copy of the repository at the release commit is at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/audit-newcomers/}; it holds the code, the protocol documents, and the paper sources, while the eight record archives (about \const{3.4}~GB) are withheld until the review ends.
238: 
239: \section*{Use of AI tools}\label{sec:ai-use}
240: AI tools were used throughout this work; responsibility for all of it rests with the author. AI coding assistants (Anthropic's Claude models) wrote most of the simulator, analysis, test and release code to the author's specifications, and drafted parts of the text; the author set the research questions, the registered hypotheses, decision rules and every amendment, and checked and revised all text. Two attackers involve language models by design: H1 is a language model acting as the focal provider, and H4 was written by a fresh session of an OpenAI model, started by the author, against the frozen defenders (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}). Drafts of the protocol, code and paper were reviewed by OpenAI GPT models and by Claude, read-only; each correction was checked against the repository before it was adopted. No AI tool is an author.
