                   Audit the Newest Identities First?
          Preregistered Simulation Tests of a Verification Rule
               and Their Limits Under Honest Turnover
                                        Anonymous submission


                                                 Abstract
          A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
      can return at once under a new identity; under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement,
      free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the
      focal identity. We study R3, a rule that audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the
      newest ones, in two registered simulation tests; the second was locked before six fresh markets
      were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash. Without honest identity turnover, the registered gain-
      and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits (B3) passes on 3 of six markets under the
      prescribed searches, all passes qualified by search flags, while R3 lowers the attacker’s measured
      gain relative to B3 on every market; R3 and a verified-history comparator, C∗ , tested in the
      second test only, are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin (C∗ minus R3:
